            <ArticleTitle>Content validity of the Dutch version of the Neck Bournemouth Questionnaire.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>386-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.math.2013.01.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1356-689X(13)00005-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Mechanical neck pain is a common human phenomenon. In most cases, no patho-anatomical lesion can be identified with the limitations of current radiological imaging. Therefore it is preferable to assess patients with neck pain with a focus on functioning and health, within a biopsychosocial model. The Neck Bournemouth Questionnaire aims to assess biopsychosocial aspects of neck pain. However, the content validity of the Neck Bournemouth Questionnaire Dutch language version (NBQ-NL) in relation to the International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF) is unknown. The purpose of the study was to examine the content validity of the NBQ-NL in relation to the health components of the ICF.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Content validity was assessed in relation to the ICF, by means of Discriminant Content Validity (DCV).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The results indicate that the NBQ-NL measures four components of the five ICF (impairment, activity, participation and personal factors).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The NBQ is a multidimensional questionnaire, representing four of the five components of the ICF.</AbstractText>
